A||||37||DecadenceR||Interesting news, kinda shocked here...||||1||
Z||000000||DecadenceR||08-04-2001||04:38 PM||||My brother was leaving for the store, and he comes into my room and says "Steve, some guy from the Army is here for you..." Needless to say i was puzzled....<P>I was in the Army National Guard, never shipped to boot camp. I date got canceled, and I was lied to so I went to my recruiter and wanted to sign out. Well needless to say, I was lied to again because i found out today that I'm not signed out. Matter of fact, I ship to Ft. Benning, Georgia on the 31st of this month, for a term of 16 weeks.<P>I'm kind ashocked, but kinda half excited too. I really have to go through with this, or I have to get a dishonorable discharge, which would not be a very good thing. It wiould prevent me from doung too many things.<P>Well, that was my saturday, how about everyone else?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||08-04-2001||04:55 PM||||Sorry to hear that if you don't want to do it... it won't be too bad...<P>But get used to getting lied to. That is just part of military life. I could go into the long story of mine but won't. Suffice it to say that they will tell you anything to get you to do what they want you to do...<P>Look on the bright side.. at least you aren't going to Ft. Lost In The Woods, Missouri! hehe<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Urchin||08-04-2001||05:06 PM||||Wow.  Talk about an unexpected turn of events.  Good luck with it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DecadenceR||08-04-2001||07:11 PM||||Thanks guys. Funny thing is I was talking recently about going back for it. I have no girlfriend, no job, nothing to reallly keep me from doing it but the usual nervousness. So I guess it's pretty good timing.<P>All in all i guess it's worked out for the best, but I just wish I would have had more time before I was leaving. I wouldn't have known at all if I wasn't at home....||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||08-04-2001||08:25 PM||||Getting out of the military is as easy as 1-2-3!!  All that is needed is a credit card and a Victoria's Secret catalouge!  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||08-04-2001||08:57 PM||||End of the world as you know it huh? Good luck. Remember: last order first-first order last. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||08-04-2001||10:17 PM||||i joined for pretty much the same reasons. not too many regrets i guess. just make sure that's the last time you volunteer.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 08-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||EdsB52||08-05-2001||03:33 AM||||Actually if you spoke with a real attorney (non-military) you might find that you can get out of it based on misrepresentation. There were guys in basic (Air Force) that got out because they didn't like it and couldn't conform. We were told that they had no record of a discharge at all (as far as conventional discharge classifications) if they didn't make it 6 months in the military. Again, if you don't want to go in, then look in to it. If you do want to go in the Army, then good luck and have a good time!<P>ps: Be all you can be <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||maryjane||08-05-2001||04:09 AM||||I believe you are correct Ed, I've heard this even holds true for the Marines now days. The military presently looks for more than just "cannon fodder", but if a man or woman isn't cut out for it, it is a waste on both sides to try to make them stay. The training money can be better spent elsewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||08-05-2001||10:37 AM||||Hey - be glad your not signed up to be an organ donor:<P>"Steve - theres a doctor here from the hospital - he says he's here to pick up your liver..."<P>Steve: "the lady at the DMV said they only collect after you die"<P>Doctor: "Do you believe EVERYTHING they tell you at the DMV?!"||||reg||11
Z||000010||DecadenceR||08-05-2001||12:43 PM||||Well it's only the National Guard, so I'm not so worried. 16 weeks and I'll be back home with nothing else to worry about. And I get my $200,000 life insurance policy, free college, and all that. I just have to deal with lotsa push-ups and yelling for 4 months. Anyone have any tips? Other than never volunteer for anything? I already got that from previous drills....<P>Thanks for the tip about court guys. but unfortunately because of time constraints, I'd still be discharged. See, I've been in for almost 2 years. And if you don't sip for basic by the time you're at the 2 year mark, you're dishonorably discharged. So byt he time I got a lawyer and got paperwork filed, I'd be pretty well past that mark.<P>Just everyone miss me a whole lot, ok???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fierokid87||08-05-2001||12:59 PM||||We will all miss you man.  The forum will not be the same with out you.  Make sure you leave me the Fiero so I can take goo care of it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="BLOODY"> Fierokid's  Web site<BR>http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero<P>AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000012||Stage-2GT||08-05-2001||03:00 PM||||  D, Don't feel to bad. What you are doing is a noble thing. Go ahead and go through with what you signed on for and keep your integrity intact. This country needs more people that aren't always looking for the easy way out. Matter of fact, if it were not for those who have served this country in both peace and war, where might we be today. There have been a lot of sacrifices, some needless, to ensure that we are "FREE" to do the things we do. There's no shame in serving your country.  Remember, it's not only your right, it's your duty.<P>S2GT <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>"If Hate Was Money, I Would Own The World"||||reg||14
Z||000013||2birds||08-05-2001||04:48 PM||||All this and a paycheck, too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Hope you do well.  <BR>Volunteering isn't a bad thing, just make sure you understand what you are volunteering for.  Just keep out of trouble and do the things you know you'll have to do.  <BR>The simplest things may seem to be stressed a bit too much (we call it anal retentiveness), but attention to detail is the key.  If you can't do the simple things right, you won't be trusted with the more difficult ones.||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||08-05-2001||07:08 PM||||2 things to always remember in military a) hurry up and wait & b) never ever volunteer for anything ||||reg||1
Z||000015||DecadenceR||08-05-2001||08:54 PM||||Stage-2, i was always interested in it. I just didn't like being lied to. getting a 96 out of 99 on the ASVAB, then being told that you're qualified for infantry isn't the kind of reassurance I needed. I should have looked farther into the scores and jobs and such beforehand. Funny thing was I was just talking to someone last week about signing back up.  Saves myself paperwork. LOL<P>I just wish I had a little more time to spare before hand. but it feels good that as soon as I tell people I'm going, everyone wants to see me before I go.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||FieroMontreal||08-05-2001||10:21 PM||||bye, we are gonna miss ya!||||reg||1
Z||000017||Steve Normington||08-06-2001||12:23 AM||||The only things that I know about boot camp came from <I>An Officer and a Gentleman</I> and <I>Stripes</I>, so I won't share.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Jaygee79||08-06-2001||09:49 AM||||We'll miss you.  Have fun!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ken Wittlief||08-06-2001||10:23 AM||||OK I will spill the beans on boot camp.<P>The essesence of it is that you will be brainwashed. They will take away your family  and friends, that will take away your idenity, your hair style, and clothes, your eating habits and ALL control over every aspect of your life<P> and they will expect you to conform to what they want from you - they will be your family, they will be your friend, they will be your boss, they will tell you when to eat and what to eat, when to sleep, what to wear...<P>  Understand this going in and it will be a breeze - they simply want to:<P>  see if you will do things their way and conform<P> see if you are marginally stable mentally - if you are a cry baby and cant handle presure<P>  see if you are a nut and are gonna go postal on them - if so they want you to snap in boot camp, not on a battle field when they need you.<P>  One of the funniest things I saw in boot camp (coast guard) was a Chief chewing out this big 18 year old guy (for nothing *), and he realized he was starting to get to the kid, then he asked him, "Does your mother know where your are? Whats her phone number? Im gonna call her up right now and tell her what a worthless piece of S%$& she raised for a son..."<P>  and the kid starts bawling, tears all over the place "No dont call my mother - I'll try harder - dont call my mom..."<P>  I dont know how the Chief did it without bursting out laughting - cause its all an act - its all a test<P> * BTW they WILL jump on your case sooner or later, it doesnt matter how well you do, or how good you are - they will find a reason to chew you out and to say you are the worst  SOB that ever came into bootcamp - Expect it!<P>   So now that you know the secret - play along with the game, remember its only 16 weeks, and after bootcame the rest of the service is totally different. For at least half the time you are there you will wish you are dead - but after you get through it you will feel like you have accomplished something.<P>  [and if you want to find some peace and quiet, a sanctuary if you will, most boot camps have a chapel or church of some kind - find your way down there and tell the chaplan you want to sing in the choir, or play an instrument during services - its the one place in boot camp where they will leave you alone and treat you like a normal person]||||reg||10
Z||000020||DecadenceR||08-06-2001||12:01 PM||||My friend rhat went to the airforce was like "Are you religious?" I said no... She said "Whether you believe in God or not, you will be there..." when i asked why, she told me ti is the ONLY place that you can go to relax. She said she went to chruch whever they called for it. LOL<P>I know I can handle it physicaly. I may not be the biggest, strongest, but I'm fit and I've seen people in worse condition make it. I'm just hoping I'll be able to overcome my usual attitude. I have a bad habbit of talking back and saying what's on my mind. especially when I have someone yelling in my face. I mean, I'm sure I'll be able to control it, but that's my main doubt.||||reg||1
Z||000021||JSocha||08-06-2001||12:22 PM||||Me, I don't know whether to say good luck (when you weren't expecting this) or sorry to hear about your situation.<P>Anyways, I do hope all goes well and I as one look forward to when you can get back and share.<P>Me, I would never "electively" join any military outfit.  <P>I don't like to fight, and although I have several rifles, I was like Richard Dean Anderson in McGyver as I hate them.  However, when you live out in the country, you need them for rabid animals that may stray into the yard or on the road.<P>Anyways, on top of it, my knees are hailed since the age of 13, my hip and shoulder joints are deteriorating, my health...well...just plain ass sucks, I'm getting old that they I feel would just throw me out to pasture for target practice and shoot me to put me out of my misery.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Just don't want to take that chance.  hehehehe<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||12
Z||000022||Old Lar||08-06-2001||12:38 PM||||Whatever you do...don't mouth off (talk back) to the drill Sgt.  They will make you life a living hell.  Just yes sir, no sir.  Think of this as pledge week to a fraternity that lasts a bit longer.  Once you are in, the work is almost like any other job.<P>If you are intelligent go for additional training, as much as possible. (Not advanced infantry)   No real good jobs learning how to kill.  Lots of technical skills to be learned in the military.<P>I was drafted (no choice).  I wasn't planning on being cannon fodder, I enlisted in the Air Force and so I put my background to use, and went to schools.  I was in school for a year and a half plus.  Lots of hard work.  But the job, once the education was finished was 0700-1600, even in Viet-Nam.<P>You will learn how to take orders...Do it first, then question, not the other way around.  You will need to play the game or you will be crushed.  Resistance is frutile.<P>It is an opportunity for a new experience.||||reg||1
Z||000023||DecadenceR||08-07-2001||02:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>Whatever you do...don't mouth off (talk back) to the drill Sgt.  They will make you life a living hell.  Just yes sir, no sir</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, that's one thing I've learned. It's Yes (rank), No(rank). YOu don't call them Sir. You will quickly get *What did you call me? Did you call me sir? Do I look like a sir to you?I WORK for MY money...* and you promptly will be "beating face".....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||1FST2M6||08-07-2001||02:26 PM||||LMAO-KEN... LOL.... D@MN IT.. coke burns when it comes out of your nose... "glad your not an organ doner" <P>------<P>My friend just graduated from the Army boot camp he had a great time, learned a lot and got to play with 50 cal machine guns for a week straight!! he's heading to Aberdeen, MD for light wheeled vehicle maintenance training anything under 5tons... i've gotta deliver his car to him in oct.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Black88GT||08-07-2001||02:27 PM||||Coast Guard <I>has</I> a boot camp?<P>I love Aberdeen proving ground.  You can go see all the tanks and everything.  They have cool museums too, 30 min. drive from  Baltimore.<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||GOLDSE||08-07-2001||04:20 PM||||DecadenceR, as one who has "been there, done that" I can tell you that you have a foot up on the other recruits who will show up.  Especially if you have done any drill time with your Guard unit.  Best just to blend into the crowd.  I graduated boot camp years ago and the Drill Sgt. didn't even know my name the day of graduation.  Success!<P>Best of luck to you.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Ken Wittlief||08-07-2001||04:28 PM||||Coast Guard boot camp is second in difficulty only to the marines.<P>the reason I joined the Coast Guard instead of the other services, is that in peace time the Coast Guard has real duties to perform, they dont just sit around waiting and training for a conflict to break out.<P>but in the time of war they fall under the dept of the Navy.<P>When the navy gives a ship to the Coast Guard, if the navy had a 300 man crew, the CG will operate the same ship with a 100 man crew, and do more things than the Navy did with it. <P>Out on the water, a Coast Guard rescue boat is like a combination of an ambulance, firetruck, police car, paddy wagon, tow truck, pollution control unit, coroners wagon... you name it, they are responsible for it.<P> So more so than any other service, the Coast Guard needs people that can perform under stress without flaking out. Their boot camp is very rigorous.<BR>  <BR> (and unlike the Navy - if you dont pass the extensive swimming/water survival tests - you dont graduate from boot camp - they boot you out).||||reg||1
Z||000028||DecadenceR||08-07-2001||08:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>I love Aberdeen proving ground.  You can go see all the tanks and everything.  They have cool museums too, 30 min. drive from  Baltimore.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I LOVED that place when I was little. My father and grandfather took me there. I'll always remember that tank witht he 6inch deep gouges from the shells...<P>GoldSE - Hopefully I'll come back with the same rate of success!!! Yes, fortunately I have done some drill, so I have a basic understanding of some things. But as always, i still have a LOT to learn.||||reg||1
Z||000029||mrfiero||08-09-2001||03:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DecadenceR:<BR><B> getting a 96 out of 99 on the ASVAB, then being told that you're qualified for infantry isn't the kind of reassurance I needed. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I scored a 95 on the ASVAB and a 37 on the Navy's Nuke test.  I joined the Navy 10 years ago as a Nuke......went to bootcamp and "A" school in Orlando.  All the time they filled my head with a lot of nonsense about Nuke's being the top 2% of the Navy, etc.  I was excited since I would start out as an E-3 (in bootcamp) and would be an E-5 within 2 years.....even E-7 (Chief Petty Officer) could be achieved before I was 28 years old. <P>What they didn't tell me was there was a 75% attrition (sp?) ratio for the Nuke program.......meaning at least 75% of those in the program would not finish the 2 years worth of schooling.   I, of course, was one of those 75%......I never made it out of "A" school.  That, in itself, wasn't the biggest letdown......what really pissed me off was the Navy's refusal to send me to another "A" school (non-Nuke).  I went to the fleet as an undesignated striker.  This meant that I had to literally beg for a rating from a department head once on a boat.  In the meantime I would do all of the sh*t jobs......so much for being the "top 2%".<P><BR>I won't bore you with the gory details, but I mangaed to get discharged after a little over a year.  I joined for a specific job and wound up painting submarines and "swabbing" the deck......that really sucked!<P>I know your situation is different, since you'll be in the Reserves, but still heed everyone's advice so far......DON'T VOLUNTEER FOR ANYTHING!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>NAVY=Never Again Volunteer Yourself (a lesson I learned too late). <P>Good luck.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||84Bill||08-09-2001||03:38 PM||||Trust me man you will look back on your days in the army and remember how much fun it really was. I still remember my DI's names Seroice, Baylock and Fuqua, three of the meanest nastiest SOB's you would ever meet and that was in 86. YEAH they busted our stones BIGTIME but in the end they were proud of us because we made it through. <P>I Have Sergent Seroice's whistle that he threw into the crowd just before graduation and I swear it has notches carved into it for everyone that quit from our squad. LOL I still remember the entire B35 company chant.<P>All you have to do to get to the end of boot camp is win the battle against yourself, it's all a mind game and they don't mind at all  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> You WILL do more before 9:00 am than most people do ALL day.<BR>So get yourself squared away, ruck up and lock step because when your 1 week from getting back on the block you will know what I'm talking about. <BR>Good luck Private!||||reg||1
Z||000031||2birds||08-09-2001||07:57 PM||||mrfiero is close to the mark on the nuke program.  The Navy Nuclear Field attrition rate is really high, but so are the SEALS, Airborne, Spec Ops, and all the other "elite" programs the different services have.  It comes naturally to some people, but I had to bust my tail harder than a lot of other guys in the nuke program.  I barely made it out of the training pipeline, but once I got to the fleet I saw that I was part of the whole team, and I have been fairly successful.  A lot of it is how bad you want it and how much crap you are willing to give and take to get there.<BR> <BR>If you want to succeed in the military, you have to work hard, even if it means volunteering for a few things now and then.  That's how you get out of being just a painter and cleaner.  (If not, someone will get "volunteered" anyway.)  On a submarine, we all sling a paint brush or a broom, or climb into a bilge to clean it now and then.  I have no problem with being an overpaid janitor, but I also value the experience and knowledge that my primary job of maintaining/repairing electrical systems and operating the power plant brings with it.  <P>Either that or I'm just brainwashed... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The military isn't for everybody, but if you want to work hard, there is opportunity.<P>Of course, you can always just do the bare minimum and still get the college money, but prospective employers may want to know about your military background, just like any other previous job.  I'm not saying you have to set the world on fire, but at least be positive about it as long as you are getting paid to do a job.||||reg||1
Z||000032||DecadenceR||08-10-2001||01:40 PM||||Thanks guys.<BR>I KNOW I can do it. And I WILL do it. <BR>I just hope I come back with success stories, instead of "Guys, I did the DUMBEST thing..."||||reg||1
Z||000033||84Bill||08-10-2001||02:22 PM||||Dec, My best advice to you is no matter how hard it gets you CAN make it. Just remember all the guys that went before you did it and you can too.<BR> It's not a cake walk but if you keep your stuff squared away and always put in 100% you will likely just be tired 100% of the time   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Heheh I fell asleep standing up once, the sound of the air whisteling past my ears woke me in time to brake my fall   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>It's going to be the most hellish fun you will ever have and you will never forget it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>Here is some helpfull stuff to know.<BR>------------<BR>Don't be the last one out of the barracks to formation...... Unless you are willing to throw lint on your rival squads floor or mess up a rack or two  so your squad wins the cleanest barracks ribbon or just to watch them get dogged like a mofo Tehehehe   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>The snack machines are (usually) not all that far from trash dumpsters so taking out the trash can be advantageous   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>hookup with good "buddy" that shares your evil views and can check your back eg. <P>Never even flintch when at attention. Those DI's see everything!<P><BR>It will take about 2 to 3 weeks before your squad begins to gell into a unit and when it does<BR>Come up with a cadence for your squad, we made one for ours and included our DI's names and it went like this.<BR>Momma Momma cant you see.. What the Army done to me. <BR>Used to hang out on the block now I'm marching with Baylock ect ect, you get the idea. The DI's love that stuff<P>Key words<BR>BEAT YOUR FACE!! (Get down and start pushing)<BR>The Block        (Home)<BR>Jody             (The person your girl is seeing now that your gone) <BR>Squared Away     (everything is ok)<P><BR>Hang in bro <p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 08-10-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000034||Ken Wittlief||08-10-2001||04:34 PM||||we were marching off to dinner one evening without our CO, and we started chanting the 'marching song' from the castle guards in the wizard of Oz.<P>  Some officer was not amused and chewed us out for a good ten minutes.<P>  [but it was worth it - its sounded pretty cool]||||reg||1
Z||000035||2birds||08-10-2001||09:25 PM||||I went to boot camp in Great Lakes back in '85, before we were politically correct and mixed gender.  <BR>Our cadences have been cleaned up quite a bit since then, but I also haven't marched in formation sice 1986, either.  One of the benefits of being in the Sea Services. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000036||DecadenceR||08-11-2001||05:51 PM||||I've always wanted to go to boot camp, just for the purpose of... Boot camp. I always saw the commercials for the army and all the army movies and stuff, and boot camp looks almost like a large playground. Except it's more yelling and a lot more work. But I really can't wait.<P>Bill- I already know the beat face term all too well. Beat face, kiss the ground, push dirt....<BR>I made the mistake at drill once of getting up before I asked the DI's permission. Long story short, I was on ther base for less than 20 minutes and already had done 60 pushups plus had jello arms for the next hour and a half...||||reg||1
Z||000037||Mach10||08-11-2001||06:43 PM||||Well, as much as I *****  and complain about the various military conflicts globally, I do believe that a country must have a large, well-trained standing army. There's more to the military that shooting things. We had a large division out to Winnipeg when the Red broke it's banks. I was a pretty decent sand-bag-slinger, but watching a line of 30 or so olive drab soldiers, 5 paces apart heaving the 50lb bags faster than we (civilians) where chucking the 20lb bags was comforting. That and the heavy machinery they brought in to help. Would have lost a lot more houses without 'em. Good for you. All of the people I work with that are ex-military and  reserve said that it was one of the best parts of their lives. I don't expect it would be any different. Good luck!||||reg||7
